{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does substance use predict who nominates whom as a friend in American Indian (AI) majority reservation schools, as it often does in racially heterogeneous schools? The authors distinguish sending nominations, receiving them, and befriending peers with similar use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Consequently, we expect that substance use will show weaker sender,\nreceiver, and homophily effects in AI-majority schools than observed in heterogeneous populations.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do students reciprocate friendships and befriend friends of friends, and do nominations concentrate around a few students or remain more evenly distributed?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Key endogenous mechanisms include reciprocity, transitivity, and degree-related effects. Critically, their prevalence and strength are not culturally neutral but are\nshaped by the social norms and relational expectations of a community [25].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore explore the nature of degree effects, whether they indicate preferential\nattachment or egalitarian constraints, within these specific AI adolescent contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Are depressive symptoms associated with receiving fewer nominations, initiating fewer friendships, or having friends with similar symptoms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\ntherefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily—\noperate in AI-majority school contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Where AI identity is widely shared, does the strength of a student's identification with their heritage nevertheless distinguish whom they nominate, who nominates them, or whom they resemble among their friends?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Alternatively, because AI identity is largely shared in\nthis population, its predictive power for tie formation may be limited. Given the exploratory nature of this investigation, we\ndo not formulate directional hypotheses but examine sender, receiver, and homophily effects empirically.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do established patterns of friendship within genders and grades recur in these schools, and do students also cluster by sexual orientation?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substantively, gender and grade homophily are among the most robust findings in adolescent network research [9, 12]; we expect\nthese patterns to replicate in AI-majority schools, where gender- and grade-segregated activities similarly organize peer\ninteraction.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily has received limited attention in the literature, but documented social stratification along sexual identity lines in adolescent populations suggests\nthat it may likewise structure friendship ties [9].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "In the three schools studied, existing friendships and shared demographic characteristics were much better guides to friendship patterns than substance use. Depressive symptoms and strength of AI identification also did little to distinguish who was friends with whom.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Similarly, depression and AI identity were not\nsubstantively associated with friendship formation. These findings reveal a distinct\nrelational ecology within AI-majority reservation schools, where peer ties are organized around structural and demographic commonalities rather than risky behaviors.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reservation schools should not be treated as interchangeable when designing prevention programs: even on the same reservation, the schools differed in how friendships were organized.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This heterogeneity carries practical implications: network-based interventions for substance use prevention may need to be calibrated to\nschool-specific relational ecologies rather than applied uniformly across reservation settings.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors deliberately selected two tribal schools serving villages of different sizes and a private preparatory school on one Northern Plains reservation. Of 263 survey respondents in grades 9 and 10, 247 had complete network data.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our selection included a tribal school\nin one of the most geographically isolated and smallest villages (school A), another larger tribal school in a more populous\nvillage (school B), and a private, college preparatory school situated near the largest village in the area (school C).",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Specifically, 72% of eligible students completed surveys in\nSchool A (n = 52 out of 72), 76% in School B (n = 140 out of 184), and 84% in School C (n = 71 out of 84), for a total sample\nsize of 263.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the 247 participants with complete network data, item non-response was limited to the substance use variables.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional survey linked student attributes to directed friendship nominations. Students used a school roster to name up to ten close friends; the analysis retained nominations between responding ninth- and tenth-graders.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey consisted of two primary sections: the first collected demographic information, measures of AI identity, depression, and substance use; the second collected directed friendship nominations.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Students were\nasked to “select up to 10 friends you are closest with in grades 7th-12th at your school.” School rosters within the Network\nCanvas software assisted identification. For analysis, we focused on ties among responding 9th- and 10th-grade students,\nstored in a square adjacency matrix for each school.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Community and youth advisory boards reviewed the survey measures before fieldwork.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All measures were reviewed for cultural appropriateness and\nrelevance by a Community Advisory Board and a Youth Advisory Board from the participating communities prior to finalization [33].",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The substance measures recorded whether students had ever used alcohol, cigarettes, or marijuana, rather than treating frequency of use as the predictor. The cigarette question excluded ceremonial tobacco use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants’ use of alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana was\nmeasured through binary variables (yes = 1; no = 0), derived from self-reported data in the survey’s initial section.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Have you ever had a drink (more than a sip) of alcohol, such as beer,\nwine, or hard liquor like vodka?”, “Have you ever smoked a cigarette? (Do not include ceremonial use of tobacco.)”, and\n“Have you ever used marijuana (also called weed, pot, chronic, or nug, including edibles)?”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Six CES-D questions measured depressive symptoms; six adapted ethnic identity items measured affirmation and belonging, with wording specific to the participating Tribe.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey also included a measure of depression, utilizing the Center for Epidemiologic\nStudies Depression Scale (CES-D) [35–38]. This section of the survey consisted of six questions, each probing different\nfacets of depression, such as persistent sadness, feelings of failure, loneliness, and the occurrence of crying spells.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The measurement of AI identity was assessed using six items adapted from the Affirmation and Belonging\nsubscale of the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure (MEIM) [39].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "item wording was adapted in partnership with the study’s Community and Youth Advisory Boards, with generic references\nto “TRIBE” replaced by the specific tribal identifier.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The models used grade, gender, and sexual orientation both to describe friendship within demographic groups and to adjust estimates for the focal attributes. The orientation measure distinguished straight from non-straight respondents.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Given\nthe low prevalence of responses outside of the straight category, and to simplify the model, we categorized sexual\norientation as straight and non-straight.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "As noted in the Introduction, the homophily terms capture substantively meaningful patterns of friendship formation along demographic lines, which we report and interpret as\nreplication benchmarks.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "serve as adjustment variables that account for demographic sorting in friendship nominations, so that the focal attribute\neffects can be interpreted net of these compositional forces.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For each school, the authors fitted a base exponential random graph model (ERGM) accounting for dependence among friendships, then three additional models separating sending, receiving, and similarity for each substance. An open two-path term distinguished opportunities for friends of friends to meet from the tendency for them actually to be friends.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This included a base model containing structural terms (reciprocity, transitivity [GWESP], degree distributions [GWIDeg, GWODeg], and a two-path term) and core substantive variables\n(demographics, depression, AI identity).",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Subsequently, we estimated separate models for each substance (alcohol,\ntobacco, marijuana) that added sender (nodeofactor), receiver (nodeifactor), and homophily (nodematch) effects for the\nspecific substance use variable.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Following Snijders et al. [28], we also included a two‑path term to separate the tendency for\nopen two‑step sequences from triadic closure, thereby allowing the GWESP coefficient to more cleanly capture transitivity.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Average marginal effects expressed associations as changes in friendship probability, so the authors could compare their magnitudes across schools rather than compare raw ERGM coefficients.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "AMEs represent\nthe average change in the predicted probability of a tie formation associated with a one-unit change in a predictor variable, holding all other variables constant. This provides an interpretable, population-averaged effect size that is directly\ncomparable across the three school networks [43].",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For missing substance-use responses, the authors inferred use from peers who had nominated the respondent. They also repeated the analysis without those cases; some significance levels changed, but not the substantive conclusions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "If over half of a respondent’s incoming friendship nominations (i.e., peers who had nominated them as a friend)\nindicated peer perception of them engaging in a specific substance use behavior (response ≥ 2, corresponding to ‘a few\ntimes a year’ or more), the respondent was classified as a user (1) for that variable;",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "sensitivity\nanalyses that exclude these cases entirely leave the direction of every focal effect unchanged and reverse no substantive conclusion, although a small number of coefficients—concentrated in School B, where the excluded cases are most\nnumerous—cross conventional significance thresholds, consistent with the reduced sample size",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A separate check removed depression, identity, and demographic covariates. The weak substance-use results persisted, arguing against overadjustment as their explanation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we estimated separate ERGMs containing only structural terms and the\nsubstance use variables (alcohol, tobacco, marijuana).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "The results (see Supporting Information, Table S2 in S1 File)\nwere consistent with the full models: substance use showed no robust or consistent association with sender, receiver, or\nhomophily effects across schools, while the structural processes (reciprocity, transitivity, degree effects) remained significant.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked fit by comparing each observed school network with 100 networks simulated from each fitted model.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Goodness-of-fit was assessed by comparing the observed network to 100 networks simulated from each fitted model\n(Figs S1–S12 in S2 File): on degree distributions (indegree and outdegree), edgewise and dyadwise shared partner distributions, geodesic distances, and the triad census.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "In all 12 models, the observed network statistics fell within the central\nrange of simulated distributions, indicating adequate model fit.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Mutual nominations were the norm in all three schools. Even after accounting for open chains of friendships, friends of friends were especially likely to be friends themselves.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity rates were high across all schools (93.3–97.7%), indicating that mutual\nfriendship nominations were the norm.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, the positive coefficients for closed triads (transitivity) and negative coefficients for two‑path confirmed that triadic\nclosure—the process by which friends of friends become friends—is a fundamental organizing principle within these AI\nadolescent networks.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Sending and receiving nominations followed different patterns: a subset of students sent disproportionately many nominations in every school, but only the private preparatory school showed a comparable concentration of popularity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The negative and significant coefficients for geometrically\nweighted outdegree (GWODeg) across all schools indicate a centralized structure in outgoing ties, meaning a subset\nof adolescents initiated a disproportionately high number of friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "For incoming ties (indegree), a\nsignificant negative coefficient was observed only in School C, indicating a centralized popularity structure in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students consistently befriended peers of the same gender and grade. Friendships also clustered by sexual orientation, though the strength of that pattern varied.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Gender and grade homophily were consistently\nsignificant across all schools (AME = 0.008–0.058, all p < 0.001), indicating that shared gender identity and grade level are\nprimary axes of friendship formation, as extensively documented in the broader adolescent literature [9,12].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily was also significant and positive (AME = 0.002–0.028), an understudied pattern that suggests meaningful\nsocial stratification along lines of sexual identity even in these small, relatively homogeneous school settings.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use did not consistently predict popularity, nomination activity, or similarity between friends. The clearest exceptions were clustering by alcohol use in School B and more nominations sent by alcohol users in School C. Marijuana clustering in B was marginal; tobacco clustering was absent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The only exceptions, alcohol\nhomophily in School B (AME = 0.0039, p < 0.01) and an alcohol sender effect in School C (AME = 0.015, p < 0.05), were isolated and did not replicate across schools or substances.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "Marijuana use exhibited homophily in School B (AME = 0.0020, p < 0.10), while tobacco use showed no\nhomophily in any school.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Neither depressive symptoms nor strength of AI identification meaningfully distinguished who sent or received nominations. Similarity in identity did not predict friendship, and similarity in depression had at most a tiny association.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Depression sender and receiver effects were substantively negligible across all schools;",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity was largely unassociated with sending or receiving ties,\nwith only a borderline positive receiver effect in School C.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Homophily based on AI identity was\nnon-significant, and depression homophily was negligible in magnitude (statistically detectable only in Schools B and C;\nAME ≤ 0.001).",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Three schools on one reservation do not establish a general pattern for AI youth on other reservations or in racially integrated schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent\nonly three schools on one reservation. The history, policies, geography, and culture of this reservation all intersect with\nnetwork formation and maintenance patterns. Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools,\nwhere Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The contrast with non-AI schools is indirect: prior studies supply benchmarks, not a contemporary comparison group matched for school size and rural location.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools\nwould strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and\ncontemporary data) was not feasible.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A snapshot of two grades cannot show developmental change or what happens to friendships during school transitions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The literature highlights the role that school transitions (elementary to middle to high school) play in adolescent\nnetworks, but our cross-sectional design imposes a fundamental interpretive limit: a single, simultaneous observation\nof the network and of individual attributes cannot capture changes in network formation and behavior over time.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The models cannot tell whether students choose similar friends or become similar through friendship. The reported associations do not establish causation in either direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends\nwith similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as\nevidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The demographic adjustment terms describe sorting, not independent causal effects of gender, grade, or orientation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients\nas independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation: the adjustment set was chosen to support the interpretation of the focal predictors, and a single adjustment set is not generally appropriate for estimating every coefficient in the\nmodel.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Peer reports used to fill missing substance-use data may reflect reporters' own behavior, potentially making friends look more alike than they are.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus, in which adolescents overestimate\nfriends’ engagement in behaviors they themselves perform—and perceived friend use may diverge from friends’ own\nself-reports [34].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "imputed values could in principle introduce or amplify apparent behavioral similarity among connected adolescents,\nif peer perceptions are shaped by reporters’ own behavior or by friendship-group norms.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The null identity result concerns measured affirmation within school friendships. It does not establish that identity is unimportant in family or kinship networks, or where AI students are a minority.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, kinship networks and familial relationships—which operate largely outside the school-based friendship system we\nmeasured—may be the primary arena where AI identity shapes social ties [27].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Future research should examine whether AI identity effects emerge in more racially\nheterogeneous school settings, where Indigenous students constitute a minority and identity affirmation may become a\nmore salient basis for in-group solidarity.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Differences among schools suggest contextual explanations but do not settle how community conditions or tribal governance shape ties, or how existing friendships can best be used in prevention.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Findings indicating differences in social networks, behaviors, and friendship formation across\nthe three schools also suggest the need to investigate how community contexts influence these factors.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "More qualitative research is needed to better understand AI adolescent networks and assess how\none can leverage existing patterns of friendship relations in interventions.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "we encourage future research to explore the role of\nlocal governance around these issues and the implications they might have on adolescent social network formation and\nrelated interventions.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend adolescent social network analysis to a setting where AI students are the majority. They retain its account of structural dependence and interpersonal similarity while treating the strength of those mechanisms as culturally and institutionally contingent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation\nschools may alter these patterns.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Taken together, these findings suggest that while structural endogenous\nprocesses are universal, their specific parameters are calibrated by institutional context.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors revise rather than reject network-based prevention: reciprocity and clustering remain promising foundations, but they challenge the premise that friendships form around risky behavior and propose investigating strengths-based connections instead.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These endogenous factors are\nfundamental principles utilized in social network interventions for adolescent substance use, suggesting a promising direction for AI social network interventions [45].",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthat this assumption does not align with our findings, it becomes imperative to investigate culturally relevant alternatives\nto understanding of how substance use relates to friendship formation.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Gaining insight into these alternative predictors is\ncrucial for developing innovative, effective strategies for substance use prevention tailored to AI youth that may center on\nstrengths-based connections rather than social ties rooted in risky behavior.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Rees, Freng, and Winfree found that AI students had fewer reciprocated friendships than White students in heterogeneous schools. The authors build on that descriptive finding by modeling reciprocity and other network processes within AI-majority schools, not by attempting to reproduce the racial comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "One study that did focus\nspecifically on AI youth network characteristics is Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17], who\nused Add Health to describe AI networks in heterogeneous school contexts, finding\nthat AI adolescents had fewer reciprocated friendships than White peers.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "However,\ntheir analysis was primarily descriptive and did not model the endogenous network\nprocesses examined here.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
